// Whether the names this run introduces take the idstd_ prefix. The standard
// library spells every parameter and local idstd_<name> (idstd's README.md),
// and a new local without it would break that rule. Two ways to know: bin/idc
// passes --fix-std when the project it was given is the standard library it
// resolved; and a unit whose every parameter and local -- at least one --
// already carries the prefix is following the rule, wherever it lives. The
// detection itself is fix_init's chain (../../../run/entry.id).
fix_std_scan() {
  int[] cnt = [0, 0];
  fix_std_rows(cnt);
  int ok = cnt[0] > 0 && cnt[1] == 0;
} return int ok;

fix_std_rows(int[] cnt) {
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len((import dvname))) {
    fix_std_row(i, cnt);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return void;

// fix_fixture builds the state a --fix function needs but not the declaration
// registry, and the scan above reads dvname (init_reg, mid/names/symbols/
// build/scopes.id) several calls below any import a case names -- so the
// reachability check a case line is held to never sees it, and fix_init's
// no-flag branch faulted on a null rather than reporting. Declared and empty
// is all the scan needs: with no rows the loop never runs and the answer is 0.
fix_std_fixture() {
  fix_fixture();
  init_reg();
} return void;
():() then fixd_std_on:(0)
():() then check_failed:(0)
